{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-84492",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of games that the team wins in a given season. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=20$ games\n\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=30%=0.3$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(20,0.3).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the variance of the number of games won is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =(20)(0.3)(1-0.3) \\\\  & =(6)(0.7) \\\\  & =4.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
